Draft Memorial by the Grand Secretaries Presenting Poems Respectfully Composed at Imperial Command on the "Picture of a Black Rabbit" [9th year of the Wanli 萬曆 era (1581)].

Legal Judgments [five items]:
Failing to Attend Court Audiences or Official Duty Sessions Without Cause;
Incumbent Officials Erecting Stone Monuments to Themselves on Their Own Authority;
Prohibiting Persons Who Have Undergone Judicial Sentencing from Serving in the Palace Guard;
Deserters Among Officers and Soldiers on Campaign or Defense Duty;
Deliberately Committing an Offense Upon Hearing That an Act of Grace and Amnesty Will Be Issued.

Third Session
Five Policy Questions

Question: The establishment of the Heir Apparent by the sovereign is recorded in ancient documents as a matter of attaching great importance to the foundation of the state. In the flourishing Zhou dynasty, within the palace they established the Six Palaces, and outside they venerated the sacrifices to the Matchmaker Deity (Gaomei 高禖), broadening the royal consorts' attendance on the king and praying for the continuation of heirs—from this, the importance attached to it can be seen. From the Qin and Han dynasties onward, the inner palace consorts were certainly not few: there were systems that increased the ranks to fourteen, those in which ranks corresponded to the titles of the outer court, and those that divided them into eight grades. Were these numbers after all any different from those of the Zhou house of Ji 姬? …
